my/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-122170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9c2da9eb2984fd4bb3d901b2be939693327ca17e0c73b2f6e4a3c19dbf1a4ad"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=1-{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)"] ["math" "x(t)={e}^{t}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)={e}^{t}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${2}^{x}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/b69783c5b926cb22a6e4321b449d006071917618da601d0ba2bfa109ffde6a0f.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${2}^{x}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{2}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9c2da9eb2984fd4bb3d901b2be939693327ca17e0c73b2f6e4a3c19dbf1a4ad"} {"question" "q-122311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{2}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={2}^{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/6ddbfef68a0b41a6ee3f11c46ff7821f7ff0dc23a16b4244b5e7a1f6579d7dd2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={2}^{2-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2-1}+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={2}^{3-x}+12 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({2}^{1-x}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9c2da9eb2984fd4bb3d901b2be939693327ca17e0c73b2f6e4a3c19dbf1a4ad"} {"question" "q-122341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=2\\sqrt{3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}+1}"] ["math" "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{2}^{3-x}+5}"] ["math" "v(x)={2}^{1-x}+3"] ["math" "v(x)={2}^{3-x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9c2da9eb2984fd4bb3d901b2be939693327ca17e0c73b2f6e4a3c19dbf1a4ad"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "2.3\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=7\\,m$ and has velocity $v=12\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/634a0680d0f159006c524b8d0eb466b2c99b0f9b5a46cf38b7cd689c9d8d6fb2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=7\\,\\text{m}$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =\\frac{9(7+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ 72 & =72+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =9(x+1)^{4/3} \\\\ v & =\\pm 3(x+1)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3(x+1)^{2/3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=7$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x+1}=t+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+2 \\\\ x+1 & =(t+2)^{3} \\\\ x & =(t+2)^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/state.json" "file_sha256" "490e1f7186b50c7e9fcf2ed3f21715b4fb9fd4df89461e9cb425e516de60b970"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9c2da9eb2984fd4bb3d901b2be939693327ca17e0c73b2f6e4a3c19dbf1a4ad"} {"question" "q-122345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/